## Oakland, California Lemon Law: What Are Your Rights?

## Learn About Your Lemon Law California Rights

Oakland’s rush hour can be a nightmare. If you’re local, you know that these streets are no place for defective cars - especially during the busiest times of the day.

Do you have a [faulty vehicle](/blog/is-my-car-a-lemon) that causes you stress on the road? Good news: you might be entitled to a lemon law settlement.

California [lemon law](/blog/california-lemon-law-101-brush-knowledge-consulting-lawyer) (the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act) was created to hold manufacturers accountable for shoddy vehicles they produce. At CLINE APC, we teach Oakland drivers what their legal rights are and how a lemon law attorney can help them.

Manufacturer mistakes entitle you to compensation if…

- The vehicle manufacturer has made at least two or more attempts to repair a warranty-covered defect; or

- The vehicle has been out of service for more than 30 days while being repaired for warranty-covered problems; and

- The vehicle has several warranty-covered problems, which were not caused by abuse of the vehicle and that impair its use, value, or safety.

## Contact a Lemon Lawyer in Oakland, California for a Free Case Evaluation

One thing to understand is that there are time constraints to filing a California lemon law claim. Your vehicle’s problems must first arise within the terms of the warranty if you are going to seek a lemon law settlement.

However, your vehicle’s issue does not need to be addressed within the first 18 months after purchase or before the odometer reaches 18,000 miles. That’s a common misunderstanding.

If the problem(s) does arise before these limits, you’ll have a strong basis for your case. Timeliness is key when it comes to seeking a repurchase or replacement of your lemon car.

When your vehicle meets the qualifications of lemon law in Oakland, California, you’re entitled to:

Manufacturer coverage of your lemon law lawyer expenses in Oakland and court costs

A full refund of the vehicle’s original acquisition price plus recoupment of incidental costs

A replacement vehicle if necessary

You may even be entitled to a civil penalty damages for the warrantor’s bad behavior.

